[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100888,
      "id": "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a",
      "title": "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "df994cfe-c4ab-54e6-9b78-55750f21c42f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=\\frac{4 y}{x},\\qquad x\\ne 0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=- 2 x^{5}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=- 10 x^{4}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=- 2 x^{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(- 2 x^{5}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=-2\\cdot 5 x^{4} \\\\ &=- 10 x^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=- 2 x^{5}$ and $y\\prime=- 10 x^{4}$ into the original equation gives expressions that are not identical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4 y}{x} \\\\ - 10 x^{4} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4\\left(- 2 x^{5}\\right)}{x} \\\\ - 10 x^{4} &\\not\\equiv - 8 x^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=- 2 x^{5}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f91c0d76-1392-5c4c-8827-b49820ccd99e",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
