      ":question/id": "a783d8f3-02ee-5946-b97f-8c98c78445dc",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=\\frac{2 y}{x},\\qquad x\\ne 0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=- 6 x^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=- 30 x.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=- 6 x^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(- 6 x^{2}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=-6\\cdot 2 x \\\\ &=- 12 x \\\\ &\\not\\equiv - 30 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=- 6 x^{2}$ and $y\\prime=- 12 x$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ - 12 x &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\left(- 6 x^{2}\\right)}{x} \\\\ - 12 x &=- 12 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=- 6 x^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
